@tarekgh are there any plans for this issue? 

By the way, this is not specific to records. If a class is used instead of a record, and that class has a constructor that receives an array as a parameter, the same behaviour (duplicate entries in the array) will happen.

There is even more bizarre behaviour, even if there is a parameter-less constructor. If the array `Config2` in example below is initialized with a default value, the result will contain the default values plus the values defined in the configuration.

Example:
```
public class MyConfig
{
   public string Config1 { get; set; } = "a"
   public string[] Config2 { get; set; } = ["A"]
}
```

```
{
  "MyConfig" : {
    "Config1": "b"
    "Config2": ["B", "C"]
  }
}
```

After binding the config to the object, the array `Config2` will have 3 strings `["A", "B", "C"]` instead of overwriting the default value with `["B", "C"]`. The value for `Config1` is overwritten with the value `"b"` as expected.

Maybe this is an old behaviour and it happens even in .NET 6.0 (I didn't check to be honest), but I can't think of a situation where appending to the array would make more sense than overwriting the array with the values in the config. For me, this looks like a bug that should be fixed as soon as possible.

@tarekgh Thank you so much for your quick reply.

Our issue is also related to the original issue reported in this ticket and it also applies to classes when there is a non parameter-less constructor without the parameter-less constructor, not just records. I only mentioned the second behaviour as something that doesn't feel natural.

I appreciate there may be cases where the example provided in [Binding config to dictionary extends values](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/compatibility/extensions/7.0/config-bind-dictionary) makes sense, but when loading the configuration the following way and `MyConfig` is a record, no one is expecting the append behaviour on collections because records are supposed to define immutable properties, i.e, the array won't grow or shrink after it is initialized.

```
public record MyConfig(string Config1, string[] Config2);

(...)

var myConfig = configuration.GetRequiredSection("MyConfig").Get<MyConfig>();
```

In my opinion, if a config value is set on a property when calling the non parameter-less constructor (which is the case when working with records), that property should be excluded from `BindProperties` logic. From what I saw in the code, it looks like the logic is parsing the config and setting the config value twice, once in the constructor and a second time when doing the `BindProperties` logic. This behaviour is innocuous while parsing and setting primitive values, but if the config value is another class with a big and complex structure, it may be costly to do it twice.

If the `BindProperties` logic is excluded for properties used in the constructor, it would fix the original issue of duplicated entries in the array/list. This is the bug that I think needs to be fixed.

Just for other people who may face this issue, we ended up with a "workaround". Instead of using arrays or lists, we use HashSet. The problem is still there (HashSet is initialized in the constructor and then the add method is called) but given the HashSet won't allow duplicate entries, it "fixes" the problem.

```c#
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using System.Text;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration; 
// requires package Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Binder

internal class Program
{
    private static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        var json = """
            {
                "MyConfig" : {
                    "values": ["v1", "v2"]
                }
            }
            """;

        var configuration = new ConfigurationBuilder().AddJsonStream(new MemoryStream(Encoding.ASCII.GetBytes(json))).Build();

        var myConfigSection = configuration.GetSection("MyConfig");
        var arrayConfig = myConfigSection.Get<ArrayConfig>();
        var listConfig = myConfigSection.Get<ListConfig>();
        var hashSetConfig = myConfigSection.Get<HashSetConfig>();

        Console.WriteLine("Array entries: "+ string.Join(", ", arrayConfig?.Values));
        Console.WriteLine("List entries: " + string.Join(", ", listConfig?.Values));
        Console.WriteLine("HashSet entries: " + string.Join(", ", hashSetConfig?.Values));


    }
}

public record ArrayConfig(string[] Values);
public record ListConfig(List<string> Values);
public record HashSetConfig(HashSet<string> Values);
```

Output
```
Array entries: v1, v2, v1, v2
List entries: v1, v2, v1, v2
HashSet entries: v1, v2
```

