> > Edit: I actually found a workaround that makes the List itself private and exposes a ReadOnly version as a property.
> 
> @Blackclaws can you share the workaround? I'm curious about how it works. Wouldn't the second call fail when trying to add elements to the ReadOnly list?

It doesn't try to add elements because it is read only.

```csharp
public record TestClass {
    public TestClass(List<ComplexClass> portConfig) =>
            _portConfig = portConfig;

    private List<ComplexClass>          _portConfig { get; } = new();
    public  IReadOnlyList<ComplexClass> PortConfig  => _portConfig.AsReadOnly();
}
```

Another workaround (Tested in dotnet 8 / C#12 only)

Replace

    public record MyOject(List<string> MyList, string SomethingElse);

with

    public class MyObject
    {
        public required List<string> MyList { get; init; }
        public required string SomethingElse { get; init; }
    }

This gets you around uniqueness requirements while maintaining the same data invariants as a record.
